---
import StoryLayout from "../../layouts/StoryLayout.astro";
import StoryChapter from "../../components/StoryChapter.astro";
import CaseJourney from "../../components/CaseJourney.astro";
import ArchitectureDiagram from "../../components/ArchitectureDiagram.astro";

const policy = [
  {
    key: "search",
    label: "Search",
    text: [
      "Type part of a journal, publisher or funder and suggestions appear. Pick one and you land on its policy. Behind it is PostgreSQL, not a separate search engine, which I'll come back to.",
    ],
    caption: "Typing “Nature Commun” and landing on the journal's policy.",
  },
  {
    key: "help",
    label: "Help pages",
    text: [
      "I set up the help, FAQ and API pages so the product owners and content team could run them without a developer. They write Markdown in a headless Directus. The site parses it into a tree and renders that with React, so editors own the words and the site owns how they look.",
    ],
    caption: "The help pages, then the open access compliance topic.",
  },
  {
    key: "data",
    label: "Data quality",
    text: [
      "A policy is only as good as the record behind it, and some identifiers, like ISSNs, were missing, muddled or typed wrong. Each record shows the policy for each version of an article, with a link to report an error.",
    ],
    caption: "The Nature Communications record: an option opened, the versions filtered, and the “Report an error” link.",
  },
];

const website = [
  {
    key: "site",
    label: "Moving to Fargate",
    text: [
      "The site moved from EC2-hosted containers to AWS ECS Fargate and RDS. From the migration until I left in February 2026, it had no further downtime.",
    ],
    caption: "jisc.ac.uk today: the Solutions menu, then the latest news. The hosting is invisible from here, which is the point.",
  },
  {
    key: "publishing",
    label: "Publishing",
    text: [
      "Editors work in Wagtail and Next.js presents the site. I kept both sides working through the move, so an editorial change still appeared the way it should.",
    ],
    caption: "From the content hub into a news article.",
  },
];

const chapters = [
  { id: "problem", label: "The problem" },
  { id: "open-policy-finder", label: "Open Policy Finder" },
  { id: "jisc-website", label: "The Jisc website" },
  { id: "what-happened", label: "What happened" },
  {
    id: "under-the-hood",
    label: "Under the hood",
    children: [
      { id: "elasticsearch", label: "Retiring Elasticsearch" },
      { id: "stewards", label: "The steward loop" },
      { id: "fargate", label: "Fargate and previews" },
    ],
  },
  { id: "lessons", label: "What I learned" },
];
---

<StoryLayout
  title="Jisc"
  description="Policy search on PostgreSQL, a data-steward feedback loop and help pages for Open Policy Finder, and the main Jisc website's move to AWS Fargate."
  meta={[
    { label: "Teams", value: "Sherpa · Digital Experience" },
    { label: "Role", value: "Senior Software Developer, Apr 2024–Feb 2026" },
    { label: "Stack", value: "Next.js · PostgreSQL · Directus · Wagtail · ECS Fargate · RDS · Terraform · Playwright" },
    { label: "Live", value: "openpolicyfinder.jisc.ac.uk", href: "https://openpolicyfinder.jisc.ac.uk" },
  ]}
  chapters={chapters}
  groups={[
    { id: "policy", threads: policy },
    { id: "website", threads: website },
  ]}
>
  <StoryChapter id="problem" title="The problem">
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Two services, two kinds of reader. Researchers use Open Policy Finder to check where they can publish open access and on what terms. Everyone else meets Jisc through <a href="https://www.jisc.ac.uk">jisc.ac.uk</a>, which content teams keep up to date.</p>
      <p>Researchers search with half a title, an acronym or a publisher's name, and the answer is only as good as the record behind it. Meanwhile the main website was going down at least once a day.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="open-policy-finder" title="Open Policy Finder" group="policy">
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>In the Sherpa Team I led the architecture and technical delivery of the data and search work, with two other developers.</p>
    </div>
    <CaseJourney project="jisc" steps={policy} />
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="jisc-website" title="The Jisc website" group="website">
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>The main site ran on EC2-hosted containers that ran out of memory, on top of a DDoS and a misconfigured network. I stepped into the Digital Experience Team's infrastructure work under a tight deadline, while still maintaining the publishing system.</p>
    </div>
    <CaseJourney project="jisc" steps={website} />
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="what-happened" title="What happened">
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Retiring Elasticsearch removed a daily sync between PostgreSQL and the search index, which had been eating developer time in the middle of a hard data migration. There's no benchmark on record, so I won't quote a speed-up.</p>
      <p>Other teams with similar datasets adopted the data-quality tooling and the steward loop, including a separate API team.</p>
      <p>The main website had no more downtime from the migration until I left in February 2026.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="under-the-hood" title="Under the hood" part>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>For engineers: the decisions behind the search, the data and the hosting.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="elasticsearch" title="Retiring Elasticsearch" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Open Policy Finder used to copy its records from PostgreSQL into Elasticsearch every day for fuzzy matching and ranking. Keeping the two in step was fiddly: the agreements data alone was keyed by date, year, name and ID.</p>
      <p>Elasticsearch wasn't meaningfully faster for this workload, and as I put it at the time, we didn't have a write problem. PostgreSQL met the product's real scale, so the search moved into it, with a simple cache in front for repeated queries, and the sync went away.</p>
      <p>The search still has to cope with imperfect queries and useful filters. It just does it in the database that already holds the truth.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="stewards" title="The steward loop" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Records arrived from many sources in many shapes. The pipeline ran ingest, improve, display, fix: it normalised records, flagged missing or wrongly typed identifiers with classifiers and deterministic checks, including validation against an external endpoint, and kept traces and reports.</p>
      <p>Roughly once a month, the gaps went back to the data stewards. They checked them against their own sets and fixed them at the source, so the next import came in cleaner.</p>
      <p>I defined the lifecycle around it as well: the schedule, the meetings and a runbook for issues. A better query can't supply a field that isn't in the record, so the people who own the data are part of the search.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="fargate" title="Fargate and previews" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>The move put both halves of the site, Wagtail for content and Next.js for the public pages, on ECS Fargate, with the database on RDS.</p>
      <p>Changes could be reviewed in Terraform-managed preview environments, which replaced a staging site few people used. Playwright tests checked user-facing behaviour as part of release.</p>
      <p>Two smaller things I'm fond of. When Jisc's shared Font Awesome licence limit started blocking deployments, I set up a cache and a local npm registry that several teams used. And in the Sherpa Team I prototyped an engineering harness with MCP that could inspect the codebase, query data, run checks and use the signed-in app. It stayed a prototype.</p>
    </div>
    <ArchitectureDiagram
      id="arch-jisc"
      title="The Jisc website after the move"
      summary="Wagtail manages content and Next.js presents it, both on AWS ECS Fargate, with the database on RDS. Changes are reviewed in Terraform-managed preview environments and checked by Playwright before release to jisc.ac.uk."
      width={720}
      height={290}
      groups={[{ id: "fargate", label: "AWS ECS Fargate", x: 20, y: 20, w: 440, h: 120 }]}
      boxes={[
        { id: "wagtail", label: "Wagtail", note: "editors manage content", x: 40, y: 60, w: 190 },
        { id: "next", label: "Next.js", note: "public pages", x: 260, y: 60, w: 180 },
        { id: "site", label: "jisc.ac.uk", x: 530, y: 60, w: 170 },
        { id: "rds", label: "RDS", note: "database", x: 40, y: 210, w: 190 },
        { id: "terraform", label: "Preview env", note: "Terraform-managed", x: 290, y: 210, w: 170 },
        { id: "playwright", label: "Playwright", note: "release checks", x: 530, y: 210, w: 170 },
      ]}
      edges={[
        { from: "wagtail", to: "next" },
        { from: "next", to: "site" },
        { from: "rds", to: "fargate", plain: true },
        { from: "terraform", to: "playwright" },
        { from: "playwright", to: "site", label: "release" },
      ]}
    />
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="lessons" title="What I learned">
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Search quality crosses team boundaries. A confusing result might need a query change, clearer help, or a fix to the source record, and working through that with the people who own the data is part of building the search.</p>
      <p>And a public website's reliability, its editorial workflow and its release path have to work together. Fixing the hosting meant understanding how people changed and checked the site.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>
</StoryLayout>
